ing edge ($884K vs $140K)", "No public general-election polling"], "rationale": "The open seat sits in a district that backed Republicans by double digits, and every major rater calls it Safe or Solid R while Polymarket prices the Democrat near 7%. Wes Climer has out-raised Mallory Dittmer more than six-to-one, so the rating consensus justifies a wider Republican margin than fundamentals alone suggest.", "watch": "Any late-breaking scandal, a surge of Democratic outside spending, or unexpected polling showing a single-digit race would warrant moving toward Lean R.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Open seat: Ralph Norman (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "South Carolina's 5th District is an open Republican-held seat after Ralph Norman left to run for governor. State Senator Wes Climer cleared the GOP primary unopposed and enters November with a dominant financial position, having raised roughly $884,000 to Democrat Mallory Dittmer's $140,000. Dittmer has campaigned with town halls in Chester, Lancaster, and York counties, but has no outside support on record.\n\nThe district's Republican lean of about eleven points, combined with unanimous Safe or Solid R ratings from nine expert outlets and prediction markets pricing the Democrat at roughly seven percent, points to a comfortable Climer win. With no public polling available, uncertainty rests on fundamentals, but the evidence strongly favors Republicans.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0458, "margin": -10.85}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0099, "margin": -15.0}]}